.SH DESCRIPTION
.B xudo
checks a request against root-owned rules.
.B xudo exec
runs an allowed command as root through the host's
.B sudo
or
.BR doas ,
which asks for your password.
This beta is that password path only.
The passwordless agent path is not built: the package has no
.B xudo\-gate\-nopass
and installs no
.B NOPASSWD
rule.
.PP
This release is a beta and has not been externally audited.
.SH OPTIONS
These options apply to every command.
They may come before or after the command name.
.TP
.BI \-\-rules\  DIR
Directory of rule files.
The default is
.IR /etc/xudo/rules.d .
.TP
.BI \-\-config\-file\  FILE
The settings file.
The default is
.IR /etc/xudo/config.toml .
.TP
.BI \-\-output\  FORMAT
.B human
(the default) prints prose and the decision trace.
.B json
prints the versioned decision envelope.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help.
.B \-h
is the short form.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version.
The version line says the build is a beta and not externally audited.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B explain
Show what the policy would decide, and why.
Executes nothing.
The command is a real argument vector after
.BR \-\- ;
the program is the first argument.
.B \-\-action
asks about one action id.
Every other action that accepts the same command still applies, and
overlapping actions resolve to the strictest.
.B \-\-operation remove\-path
asks about one absolute path against the
.B [[operation]]
table.
The path is UTF\-8 on this command.
.B xudo op
accepts the raw bytes.
Executes nothing.
.TP
.B why\-not
Show why one action would refuse this argv.
.B \-\-action
is required.
Executes nothing.
.TP
.B op
Run a typed operation as root, if a rule permits it.
Asks for your password through the host backend.
.B remove\-path
.I target
removes
.I target
when an
.B [[operation]]
rule names the tree that contains it.
It does not run
.BR rm .
.B \-\-dry\-run
lists the paths and removes nothing.
A
.B harness
label on the rule is parsed and checked against the registry.
It is not a grant.
Harness use is planned.
This command uses the password path only.
The passwordless gate is not in this release.
.TP
.B exec
Run the command as root, if the broker permits it.
Asks for your password through the host backend.
The program is an absolute path, after
.BR \-\- .
The command's own status is the process status.
A policy refusal happens before the command runs.
.IP
Human-path denials are not written to the invoking user's
.BR ido (1)
queue. A verified harness request matched by an
.B ask-human
rule is stored by the broker in its root-held queue.
.B xudo exec \-\-queued
.I id
claims that entry for a fresh decision against the current rules. The stored
working directory is not used; the command runs in
.BR / .
Only a human reviewer who does not own the entry can claim it. The run uses
the same scoped sudo rule as any other xudo authorization, so sudo asks for
the password. Its
.B timestamp_timeout=0
setting prevents it from reusing a sudo credential cached by an earlier
terminal login; the backend tests pin this setting in the generated rule.
The entry is marked done only after the gate's audit record for that run.
A denied password releases the claim back to pending. A rules change after
queueing denies the run. The passwordless gate is not installed.
.IP
If a human watch approval is pending, the default timeout returns at once
with exit status 18 and leaves the request pending.
Set
.B confirmation_timeout_secs
in the settings file to wait for approval; it defaults to 0 and accepts values
from 0 through 600 seconds. The settings file,
.IR /etc/xudo/config.toml ,
must be readable by the invoking user; otherwise exec fails closed with status
19. A positive timeout of n seconds starts after the broker first reports the
pending request. If the broker is silent, xudo waits at most n+5 seconds from
that point. After an approval, the consuming run request has a separate maximum
of 5 seconds. With
.B \-\-output json,
each pending update is one compact JSON object per line (JSON Lines).
.TP
.B watch
Watch for pending confirmation requests on this terminal.
The broker checks the watcher's session and terminal on each heartbeat and
answer. A line shows the canonical command, governing rule, classification,
evidence, time left, and the action's final word. Type that word to approve,
or
.B n
to deny. Approval still requires the command's password. Input typed before
the prompt is flushed; a rejected answer leaves the request pending.
.TP
.B pending
List this user's pending confirmation requests without answering them.
This command does not require a terminal.
.TP
.B freeze
Requires
.BR \-\-reason \fITEXT\fR .
Freeze the agent path with an operator supplied reason. The scope is the
caller's. A user's freeze stops only that user's own agents: it writes a
marker named by the user's uid, and root's global freeze is untouched.
Root's freeze is global and stops every user's agents. An attested agent
cannot freeze, and neither can a peer in another user namespace. Until the
passwordless agent path ships this sets the flag but changes nothing for new
requests or for human requests: every caller is unattested, and the human password path
stays available. Pending or approved requests of an enrolled agent in the
freezer's scope (every user's, for root) are denied and audited, without a
backoff strike; a human's pending request is left alone.
.TP
.B thaw
Lift the caller's own freeze and resume that scope. A user's thaw removes only
the marker for their own uid: root's global freeze stays in force, and so does
every other user's, until root lifts it. Root's thaw lifts the global freeze
and every user's marker. A non-root thaw is refused for an attested agent, an unattested caller, a
peer in another user namespace, and any caller whose uid is the account or a
user of a registered harness: being outside an agent scope does not prove a
human, because an agent can start a process outside its scope as its own uid.
Those users run
.B sudo xudo thaw
instead, which reaches the broker as root and lifts ALL freezes: the global one and every user's marker. If /etc/passwd is missing or empty (stray non-UTF-8 bytes in it are read lossily), every
xudo request refuses; this only withholds, and root can
.B touch /var/lib/xudo/frozen
to freeze or remove it to thaw. If the harness registry cannot be loaded, the
rules still decide the human path, no harness is armed, and only
.B sudo xudo thaw
lifts a freeze, since no uid can be ruled out as an agent's. A thaw that needs a password proof for ordinary
users arrives with the passwordless gate. A user who is not a harness uid runs
it as themselves; do not run it as
.B sudo
to clear a user's freeze unless root's global freeze should go as well.
.TP
.B check
Lint the loaded ruleset, then run the
.B [[test]]
tables in its rule files (see
.BR xudo\-rules (5)).
Overlapping actions are a lint warning, not a refusal: a command more than one
action accepts is refused to an agent if any of them is human-only, and
confirmed as strictly as the strictest demands.
An action that allowlists an executable the compiled-in deny-list refuses is a
lint error.
A systemctl rule that can start or stop a unit it does not name is a lint
error too.
In the JSON envelope both sit under
.BR lint :
deny-list hits in
.BR errors ,
open units in
.BR open_unit_lints .
.B doctor
uses that same field name,
.BR open_unit_lints ,
for the same rows.
Everything found is printed, and the exit status is the most severe problem:
.B 20
for a lint warning,
.B 21
for a lint error,
.B 22
for a failing test.
.B \-\-tests\-only
skips the lint, and cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-fuzz .
With
.B \-\-output json
the report is one envelope, with a result for each test.
A ruleset that will not load, an unknown key in a test table included, is
.BR 19 ,
before any of this runs.
When no loaded systemctl action accepts a lifecycle or power verb,
.B check
prints one note and does not change the exit status:
.B no service lifecycle rule: copy /usr/share/xudo/examples/20\-services\-lifecycle.toml to /etc/xudo/rules.d/ and list your units
The action id is not what decides this.
The JSON envelope carries the same sentence in
.B note
only when that verb is absent.
.IP
.B \-\-fuzz
also runs a bounded, deterministic fuzz of the ruleset: candidate commands are
derived from its own rules, byte for byte, with mutations (an argument
appended, dropped, swapped or duplicated, one byte changed, a
.BR \-\- ,
an empty, a very long or a non\-UTF\-8 argument), and each is put to the engine,
never run.
It cross\-checks the overlap report (two actions accept a command it does not
list), canonicalization (the decision changes when the argv is canonicalized
first) and appending (a deny\-list refusal that a trailing argument turns into a
permission; the agent path is not covered).
Any finding is a lint error, printed as the command both sanitized and as
hexadecimal bytes.
.B \-\-fuzz\-budget
sets how many candidates to try, 1 to 1000000 (default 20000);
.B \-\-fuzz\-seed
sets the seed, any unsigned 64\-bit number.
The same ruleset and seed give the same report.
.TP
.B list
List each action's id, tier, executable, and the file and line it came from.
When the ruleset contains typed operations and
.B \-\-tier
is omitted, the list continues with an
.B operations
section: id, operation, tree, and source.
.B \-\-tier
shows actions whose tier contains that name.
Hyphens in the name are ignored, so
.B diag\-read
matches.
The tier names are
.BR diag\-read ,
.BR svc\-control ,
.BR pkg\-install ,
.BR pkg\-remove ,
.BR sys\-tunable ,
.BR net\-filter ,
.BR critical .
No match exits
.BR 17 .
.TP
.B doctor
Report the platform, the ruleset, how this caller would be classified, and
every other path to root on this machine.
It reads as you.
What you cannot read is reported as unverified, never as absent; run it as
root as well to read what this account cannot.
.B \-\-json
prints the versioned machine-readable envelope, the same as
.BR \-\-output\ json .
.B \-\-fix
proposes a fix for each path to root, including the command a human would
run, and applies nothing.
When the rules loaded and no systemctl action accepts a lifecycle or power verb,
.B doctor
prints the same one-line note as
.BR check .
That note is not a fault, and
.B \-\-json
includes it as
.B lifecycle_note
only then.
Open unit lints are
.B open_unit_lints
in that envelope.
Each row names the action, the file, the line, and the sentence
.B check
prints.
.TP
.B config
Show the effective settings and where each value came from.
.B \-\-schema
prints the machine-readable schema instead.
.B get
prints one value, and with
.B \-\-origin
the file and line that set it, or
.B <default>
or
.BR <compiled-in> .
.B list
prints the values a file set, and with
.B \-\-effective
the defaults and compiled-in values too.
.B schema
is
.BR \-\-schema .
Only
.B \-\-config\-file
is read, as the system layer.
A missing file is the defaults, not an error.
A file that does not parse, or that contains an unrecognised key, exits
.BR 19 .
The keys are
.B confirm_human_path
(default false),
.B confirmation_timeout_secs
(default 0, maximum 600 seconds),
.B queue_ttl_secs
(default 86400, maximum 86400; a project setting may only shorten it),
.B frozen
(default false),
.B audit_sink
(default
.BR journald ),
.B color
(default true), and
.BR use_pty ,
which is compiled in as true and is refused if set.
When enabled, a rule that requires confirmation also requires an out-of-band
watch approval; the password alone does not satisfy it.
The command marks security-relevant settings and says they are never
settable from the environment.
This binary does not read the environment for them.
.TP
.B agent
Enrol, list or remove a harness that runs headless as its own account.
.B enrol
and
.B remove
need root, like
.BR install .
.B enrol
prints the three files it will write, then writes a registry entry in
.IR /etc/xudo/harnesses.d ,
a systemd drop-in that sets
.B User=
and
.B ExecStart=
for
.IR xudo\-agent@label.service ,
and an empty environment file in
.I /etc/xudo/agents
(mode 0600, root, never replaced), and enables the unit.
The program is an absolute path and is written quoted, with
.B %
and
.B $
doubled, so nothing in it is expanded by systemd.
It refuses an account that is root, has a usable password, is in
.BR sudo ,
.BR wheel ,
.BR root ,
.BR docker ,
.BR lxd ,
.BR incus ,
.BR libvirt ,
.BR disk ,
.BR shadow ,
.B adm
or
.BR systemd\-journal ,
that the scan of other paths to root finds a sudo or doas rule for, that is
already used by another harness, or that is its own reviewer; a drop-in
directory for the label that is not root's or is not empty; and a unit whose
.B User=
systemd does not report as the account.
It grants nothing: no rule names the harness, so every command it asks for
still asks for the sudo password.
.B \-\-dry\-run
prints the files and writes nothing.
.B list
shows each enrolled agent and whether its unit is active.
.B remove
stops the unit (its own stop hook voids the record), removes the drop-in and
then the registry entry, and keeps the environment file, which may hold
credentials.
.TP
.B install
Integrate xudo with the local
.B sudo
or
.B doas
backend, or remove that integration.
Needs root.
Every step is validated by the backend's own parser before anything is moved,
and only files xudo wrote are replaced or removed.
.B \-\-dry\-run
prints the plan and changes nothing.
.B \-\-show\-contents
prints the file each step would write.
.B \-\-uninstall
removes what an install wrote, for both backends, and nothing else.
On doas, which uses the last rule that matches, an install refuses (exit 19)
when a rule after xudo's own gives the xudo group
.BR nopass ,
and warns when it gives
.BR persist .
It can only see xudo's rule once installed, so the first install is checked by
.BR "xudo doctor" .
The rules this build would install all require a password.
An install is reported as verified only when the broker minted a request for
.BR "id -u" ,
the gate ran through the backend and printed
.BR 0 ,
and the backend lists the gate for a member of the group.
Anything less keeps the validated files, says
.BR "NOT VERIFIED" ,
and exits
.BR 19 .
.TP
.B audit
Verify that a hash-chained log has not been edited in place.
The default path is
.IR $HOME/.local/state/ido/log.jsonl .
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
is not read; if it is set, pass
.BR \-\-log .
The broker's own log is
.IR /var/lib/xudo/audit.jsonl .
.B \-\-against\-journal
compares file records with the journal's root-written xudo entries, which
can show a tail removed or records replaced after a cut.
An unbroken chain does not prove the log was never truncated and rebuilt.
A damaged log exits
.BR 17 .
A log that cannot be read, or a journal check that cannot run, exits
.BR 19 .
.TP
.B agentdoc
Print the block that tells an agent what the loaded rules permit.
.B \-\-format
is
.B agents
(the default,
.BR AGENTS.md ),
.B claude
.RB ( CLAUDE.md ),
or
.B codex
(Codex CLI).
.TP
.B help
Print the same text as
.BR \-\-help ,
or the help of the named command.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The request succeeded.
.B exec
returns the command's own status instead, so a command that exits 0 is the
only successful run; any other status from the command, or from the password
prompt, is that status.
.TP
.B 17
Denied by policy, or an audit log that is not intact.
.TP
.B 18
Confirmation is required, or there is no live confirmation channel.
Both mean nobody has approved the action.
An approval request still pending at the configured timeout also exits 18.
.TP
.B 19
.B xudo
could not operate and failed closed: the ruleset or the settings file would
not load, no usable backend, an install that is not verified, an audit log
that could not be read, or a status that does not fit in a byte.
.TP
.B 20
.B check
found a lint warning, and nothing worse.
.TP
.B 21
.B check
found a lint error: an action allowlists an executable the deny-list refuses,
or a systemctl rule can start or stop a unit it does not name.
.TP
.B 22
.B check
ran the rule files' tests and at least one did not hold, or could not run.
.PP
.B 17
through
.B 22
sit above the
.BR 0 / 1 / 2
band a program uses for its own failures and below
.BR 128 ,
where a shell reports a killed child as
.IR 128+signal .
A refusal is therefore distinct from a command that ran and failed, and from
a command that was killed.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/xudo/rules.d/
Rule files, loaded in name order.
See
.BR xudo\-rules (5).
.TP
.I /etc/xudo/config.toml
Settings.
Not a rule file.
.TP
.I /run/xudo/broker.sock
The broker socket.
systemd listens; one
.B xudod
serves each connection.
Mode
.BR 0660 ,
group
.BR xudo .
.TP
.I /var/lib/xudo/audit.jsonl
The hash-chained audit log the broker and the gate append to.
.TP
.I /etc/sudoers.d/xudo
Written by
.BR "xudo install" ,
mode
.BR 0440 .
Not shipped in the package.
The name has no dot and no trailing tilde, which
.B sudo
would ignore.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/xudod.socket
.TQ
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/xudod@.service
.TQ
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/xudo-pasban.service
.TQ
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/xudo-pasban.timer
The broker socket, one broker instance per connection, and the daily audit
check.
Pasban runs
.B xudo audit \-\-log /var/lib/xudo/audit.jsonl \-\-against\-journal
as root.
.SH SECURITY
Agent detection is not a security boundary.
.BR CLAUDECODE ,
.BR argv[0] ,
.BR comm ,
the command line,
.BR /proc/ pid /exe ,
and process ancestry are unauthenticated hints.
They are recorded.
Authorization does not branch on them.
An unattested caller takes the human path and is asked for a password.
.PP
Confirmation is not read from the requester's standard input, standard
output, or terminal.
No live confirmation channel means deny.
.PP
This beta has not been externally audited.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xudo\-rules (5),
.BR ido (1),
.BR sudo (8),
.BR doas (1)
